          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Sequential (Type I) vs Partial (Type III) Sums of Squares, Partial Correlations, and Adjusted R-Squared",
          "content": "In simple linear regression, there is only one explanatory variable, so the Regression Sum of Squares $\\text{SSR}$ unambiguously belongs to $X_1$. In multiple linear regression with correlated predictors ($X_1, \\dots, X_k$), the total explained sum of squares $\\text{SSR}(X_1, \\dots, X_k) = \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}^T \\mathbf{X}^T \\mathbf{Y} - n\\bar{Y}^2$ **cannot** be decomposed into unique, order-independent additive components for each individual variable unless the predictors are mutually orthogonal. Instead, statistical theory defines **Extra Sums of Squares** that measure the marginal reduction in $\\text{SSE}$ (or equivalent increase in $\\text{SSR}$) when a subset of predictors is added to an existing baseline model:\n\n$$\\text{SSR}(X_2 \\mid X_1) = \\text{SSE}(X_1) - \\text{SSE}(X_1, X_2) = \\text{SSR}(X_1, X_2) - \\text{SSR}(X_1)$$\n\nBy the Frisch–Waugh–Lovell Theorem and the orthogonal decomposition of projections $\\mathcal{C}(\\mathbf{X}_1, \\mathbf{X}_2) = \\mathcal{C}(\\mathbf{X}_1) \\oplus \\mathcal{C}(\\mathbf{M}_1 \\mathbf{X}_2)$, the projection matrix of the full model decomposes additively as $\\mathbf{H} = \\mathbf{H}_1 + \\mathbf{P}_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_2}$, where $\\mathbf{P}_{\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_2} = \\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2(\\mathbf{X}_2^T\\mathbf{M}_1\\mathbf{X}_2)^{-1}\\mathbf{X}_2^T\\mathbf{M}_1$, and the extra sum of squares is the quadratic form:\n\n$$\\text{SSR}(X_2 \\mid X_1) = \\mathbf{Y}^T (\\mathbf{H} - \\mathbf{H}_1) \\mathbf{Y} = \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}_2^T (\\mathbf{X}_2^T \\mathbf{M}_1 \\mathbf{X}_2) \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}_2$$\n\nThis distinction generates two primary sum-of-squares conventions reported in statistical software (such as R's `anova()` vs `car::Anova()` and SAS):\n1. **Sequential (Type I) Sums of Squares**: Predictors are entered in a specified sequential order $X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_k$. The sequential sums of squares partition the total model $\\text{SSR}$ additively:\n   $$\\text{SSR}(X_1, X_2, X_3) = \\text{SSR}(X_1) + \\text{SSR}(X_2 \\mid X_1) + \\text{SSR}(X_3 \\mid X_1, X_2)$$\n   Type I sums of squares always add up to $\\text{SSR}(X_1, \\dots, X_k)$, but their values depend critically on the order in which the variables are listed!\n2. **Partial / Marginal (Type III) Sums of Squares**: For each variable $X_j$, the Type III sum of squares $\\text{SSR}(X_j \\mid X_1, \\dots, X_{j-1}, X_{j+1}, \\dots, X_k)$ measures the marginal reduction in $\\text{SSE}$ when $X_j$ is entered **last** into the model containing all other $k - 1$ predictors. Type III sums of squares are invariant to variable ordering and correspond directly to the individual partial $t$-tests ($F_j = \\text{SSR}(X_j \\mid \\mathbf{X}_{(-j)}) / s^2 = t_j^2$), but they do not sum to $\\text{SSR}(X_1, \\dots, X_k)$ when predictors are correlated.\n\n### Coefficient of Partial Determination and Adjusted $R_a^2$\nThe **Coefficient of Partial Determination** $R_{Y j \\cdot (-j)}^2$ measures the proportional reduction in the unexplained sum of squares $\\text{SSE}(\\mathbf{X}_{(-j)})$ achieved by adding predictor $X_j$ to a model that already contains all other covariates $\\mathbf{X}_{(-j)}$:\n\n$$R_{Y j \\cdot (-j)}^2 = \\frac{\\text{SSE}(\\mathbf{X}_{(-j)}) - \\text{SSE}(\\mathbf{X})}{\\text{SSE}(\\mathbf{X}_{(-j)})} = \\frac{\\text{SSR}(X_j \\mid \\mathbf{X}_{(-j)})}{\\text{SSE}(\\mathbf{X}_{(-j)})} = \\frac{t_j^2}{t_j^2 + (n - p)}$$\n\nTaking the signed square root (matching the sign of $\\hat{\\beta}_j$) gives the **partial correlation coefficient** $r_{Y j \\cdot (-j)}$, which is identically the simple correlation between the FWL residuals $\\mathbf{e}_{Y \\mid (-j)}$ and $\\mathbf{e}_{j \\mid (-j)}$.\n\nFinally, in multiple regression, the ordinary Coefficient of Multiple Determination $R^2 = 1 - \\text{SSE}/\\text{SST}$ is a **monotonically non-decreasing function** of the number of predictors $k$: adding any arbitrary noise variable $X_{k+1}$ cannot increase the minimized residual sum of squares $\\text{SSE}$, so $R^2$ never decreases even when useless predictors are added. To penalize superfluous parameters, Henri Theil introduced the **Adjusted Coefficient of Determination**, $R_a^2$ (or $\\bar{R}^2$), which replaces $\\text{SSE}$ and $\\text{SST}$ by their unbiased variance estimators $\\text{MSE} = \\text{SSE}/(n - p)$ and $\\text{MST} = \\text{SST}/(n - 1)$:\n\n$$R_a^2 = 1 - \\frac{\\text{SSE} / (n - p)}{\\text{SST} / (n - 1)} = 1 - \\frac{s^2}{s_Y^2} = 1 - \\left(\\frac{n - 1}{n - p}\\right)(1 - R^2)$$\n\nAlgebraically, adding a single new predictor $X_j$ increases $R_a^2$ if and only if its partial $F$-statistic exceeds $1$ ($F_j = t_j^2 > 1$, or $|t_j| > 1$), because only when $F_j > 1$ does the reduction in $\\text{SSE}$ more than compensate for the loss of one error degree of freedom.\n\n### Reconciling Sequential (Type I) and Marginal (Type III) Sums of Squares in Applied Modeling\nUnderstanding why Sequential (Type I) and Partial (Type III) sums of squares differ when predictors are correlated prevents serious misinterpretations in applied data analysis. When two explanatory variables share substantial overlapping variation, the variable entered first in a Sequential (Type I) decomposition is credited with the entire shared variation in addition to its own unique variation, whereas the variable entered second is credited only with its incremental contribution beyond the first variable. Consequently, Type I tests are ideal when variables possess a natural hierarchical or causal ordering—such as polynomial powers where the linear term must precede the quadratic term, or nuisance baseline covariates that must be adjusted for prior to testing experimental treatments. Conversely, Type III partial sums of squares treat all predictors symmetrically by evaluating each variable as if it were entered last, making Type III tests the standard choice for assessing whether any single predictor can be dropped from a non-hierarchical multiple regression model.\n\nIn addition, comparing the unadjusted coefficient of multiple determination against Theil's adjusted coefficient of determination provides an essential diagnostic against overparameterization in small samples. When the number of columns in the design matrix approaches the sample size, the $p$-dimensional column space of the predictors spans nearly the entire $n$-dimensional observation space, forcing the unadjusted coefficient of determination toward unity even if every predictor is pure random noise independent of the response. Because the adjusted coefficient of determination divides both the residual sum of squares and the total sum of squares by their respective degrees of freedom, it penalizes the loss of residual degrees of freedom and can even become negative when a set of explanatory variables explains less variation than expected by chance alone.",
